--- short ---
Hale enters a closed virtual-reality match rendered as 1984 Bennington, Vermont. Guests cannot leave except by elimination or by completing the Daywalker Ritual, a dawn invocation at one site that requires a finite set of tokens and awards a single prize. Inside the match Guests are vampires and must feed on Hosts. Hale maps the town and feeds three night workers short of death, paying and tipping so they cannot place him. At Al's Depot Diner, waitress Cora Voss photographs the empty room and the careful customer and records a radio song as proof the night happened. Hale identifies the Ritual as locatable tokens and an invocation at Founders' Stone.

Another Guest, Anja Reeve, stacks hypnotic writes on the same three Hosts Hale is draining. Combined with his rotational feeding, the writes tear the Memory Wipe that erases each Host's night upon sleep. Cora wakes with fragments and Polaroids she does not remember taking. She matches her bruise to night nurse Denise Cole; her stack catches Ritual residue. Hale uses the stack to lift two tokens. Cora flinches at his face; he returns a Polaroid he should have kept. Rivals close the town with ultraviolet cages, dry hospital wards, a campus cult, and a heartless body in the woods. The diner locks, Denise refuses Hale's eye, and his clean rotation dies.

Cora plays a cassette that contains Hale's feeding instructions. He starts a hypnotic overwrite to take the last token, then stops. Anja adds another write and multiplies the fragments. Hale collects the last token at Founders' Stone and understands that speaking the invocation would erase every hour Cora has kept. She waits there with a live ultraviolet lamp. He withholds the invocation, gives her the tokens and the cassette, and takes a sun-scorch that will not regenerate. She hides the tokens so no Guest can win. The match stays running. After sunrise she clips a still-developing Polaroid of his face to the diner register beside her tip jar.
