--- short ---
Twenty-one days before Highkettle’s midwinter rite, apprentice Willa and master smith Orren weigh thin stock for the unfinished iron tithe at the forge-house; what she owes him for raising and craft remains unset. That night a far-slope band steals the village presentation-relic from the chest and leaves the tithe-iron. The hamlet sends Willa because Orren cannot cross the winter pass and the tithe is still short. She walks the pass on foot, then takes the relic from the sleeping lee camp by weight and craft and kills no one. The return frostbites her hands.

Six days before the rite she weighs the piece, reads a false weight and Orren’s weld, and keeps the reading. He admits the relic has been a forty-year forgery and that a hidden levy bound into it at forging is paid by his body—the only way a substitute holds the compact with the valley Hold. The levy ends only with his death or a public breaking. She cannot settle the debt. She partitions it: silence now, winter work, leaving when the pass opens in spring.

At midwinter she presents the forged relic and the thin tithe. The Hold’s steward accepts both without an inner test, and Highkettle keeps one year of pass-protection and grain-right. That night she names the weld and levy as metal; he repeats the old reasons and is not forgiven. The next morning they return to shared work that is no longer home. She sets a true bar and the balance arm comes level. Orren keeps paying the levy. The village stays ignorant that the protection dies with him. Willa will leave at the spring opening of the pass.
